{"questions":[{"question_id":"mp9wr2bi","paperTitle":"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 1 Online","year":2026,"subject":"physics","chapter":"wave-optics","chapterGroup":"optics","type":"mcq","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"content":"<p>In the List-I, four optical effects are mentioned. The physical phenomena of light which are essential to describe these optical effects are given in List-II. Choose the option which describes the correct match between the entries in List-I to those in List-II.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; border:1px solid #ccc;\">\n\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color:#007BFF; color:white;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; text-align:left;\">List-I</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; text-align:left;\">List-II</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n\n  <tbody>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(P)</strong> Colorful sky in north polar region (Aurora Borealis)\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (1) Dispersion and reflection\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(Q)</strong> Partially polarized sun light\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (2) Total internal reflection\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(R)</strong> Rainbow\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (3) Diffraction\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(S)</strong> Dark and bright fringes\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (4) Scattering of light by molecules in the atmosphere\n        \n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n     \n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n      \n        (5) Emission of radiation from oxygen and nitrogen atoms excited by charged particles\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n  </tbody>\n\n</table>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>P→5, Q→4, R→1, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>P→4, Q→2, R→1, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>P→4, Q→1, R→2, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>P→5, Q→4, R→1, S→2</p>"}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"<p><b>(P) Colorful sky in north polar region (Aurora Borealis)</b></p>\n<p>The Aurora Borealis occurs when high-speed charged particles (electrons and protons) from the solar wind collide with atoms and molecules in Earth's upper atmosphere. These collisions transfer energy to the atmospheric gases, exciting the electrons within oxygen and nitrogen atoms to higher energy levels. When these excited electrons return to their ground states, they emit photons of specific characteristic wavelengths (such as green and red from oxygen, or blue and violet from nitrogen).</p>\n<p>So, this phenomenon is emission of radiation from oxygen and nitrogen atoms excited by charged particles.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.452ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.919ex\" height=\"2.149ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3500 950\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> P → </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3000,0)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(Q) Partially polarized sun light</b></p>\n<p>Direct sunlight entering the Earth's atmosphere is initially unpolarized. However, when this light strikes air molecules and small particles suspended in the atmosphere, it undergoes Rayleigh scattering. The scattered light viewed at an angle perpendicular ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.25ex\" height=\"1.649ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -707 1436.6 729\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2218\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2218\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) to the incident beam becomes completely plane-polarized, while light scattered at other intermediate angles is partially polarized.</p>\n<p>So, it is due to scattering of light by molecules in the atmosphere.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.44ex\" height=\"2.032ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 2846.6 898\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1068.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2346.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(C) Rainbow</b></p>\n<p>A rainbow is formed when sunlight enters a spherical raindrop suspended in the air. The process involves:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 1 Online Physics - Wave Optics Question 2 English Explanation\" src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Refraction and Dispersion: Sunlight bends and splits into its constituent colors as it enters the drop due to different wavelengths having slightly different refractive indices.</p>\n<p>Reflection: The separated colors travel through the droplet and reflect off the back inside surface of the water drop.</p>\n<p>Refraction: The light exits the droplet, bending once more before reaching the observer's eye.</p>\n<p>So, during the process of rainbow formation the light undergoes dispersion and reflection.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.316ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2791.6 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-52\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1013.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2291.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(D) Dark and bright fringes</b></p>\n<p>The regular alternation of dark and bright bands (fringes) is the pattern produced by the phenomena of wave interference and diffraction. When light encounters a small aperture, obstacle, or slit system, it bends around edges (diffraction) and superimposes, generating constructive (bright) and destructive (dark) regions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the dark and bright fringes are formed by diffraction.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.11ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 2700.6 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(922.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2200.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.452ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"29.202ex\" height=\"2.149ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 12907.2 950\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(958.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2236.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2736.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3181.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4237,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(5514.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6014.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6459.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-52\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7473.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(8751,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9251,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(9695.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(600,0)\"><use data-c=\"53\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-53\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11129.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(12407.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>. \n<br><br>Thus, the correct option is (A).</p>","country":"in","difficulty":"easy","exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","isBonus":false,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"languages":["en"],"paperId":"jee-advanced-2026-paper-1-online","permalink":"pin-the-list-i-four-optical-effects-are-mentioned-the-ph-jee-advanced-physics-bpkof6dzhhvuzixx","question":{"en":{"comprehension":null,"direction":null,"content":"<p>In the List-I, four optical effects are mentioned. The physical phenomena of light which are essential to describe these optical effects are given in List-II. Choose the option which describes the correct match between the entries in List-I to those in List-II.</p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-family:Arial, sans-serif; border:1px solid #ccc;\">\n\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background-color:#007BFF; color:white;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; text-align:left;\">List-I</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; text-align:left;\">List-II</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n\n  <tbody>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(P)</strong> Colorful sky in north polar region (Aurora Borealis)\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (1) Dispersion and reflection\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(Q)</strong> Partially polarized sun light\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (2) Total internal reflection\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(R)</strong> Rainbow\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (3) Diffraction\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        <strong>(S)</strong> Dark and bright fringes\n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n        (4) Scattering of light by molecules in the atmosphere\n        \n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n     \n      </td>\n\n      <td style=\"padding:12px; border:1px solid #ccc; vertical-align:top;\">\n      \n        (5) Emission of radiation from oxygen and nitrogen atoms excited by charged particles\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n\n  </tbody>\n\n</table>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>P→5, Q→4, R→1, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>P→4, Q→2, R→1, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>P→4, Q→1, R→2, S→3</p>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>P→5, Q→4, R→1, S→2</p>"}],"correct_options":["A"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p><b>(P) Colorful sky in north polar region (Aurora Borealis)</b></p>\n<p>The Aurora Borealis occurs when high-speed charged particles (electrons and protons) from the solar wind collide with atoms and molecules in Earth's upper atmosphere. These collisions transfer energy to the atmospheric gases, exciting the electrons within oxygen and nitrogen atoms to higher energy levels. When these excited electrons return to their ground states, they emit photons of specific characteristic wavelengths (such as green and red from oxygen, or blue and violet from nitrogen).</p>\n<p>So, this phenomenon is emission of radiation from oxygen and nitrogen atoms excited by charged particles.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.452ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.919ex\" height=\"2.149ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3500 950\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> P → </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3000,0)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(Q) Partially polarized sun light</b></p>\n<p>Direct sunlight entering the Earth's atmosphere is initially unpolarized. However, when this light strikes air molecules and small particles suspended in the atmosphere, it undergoes Rayleigh scattering. The scattered light viewed at an angle perpendicular ( <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.25ex\" height=\"1.649ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -707 1436.6 729\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use><use data-c=\"30\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-30\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(1033,393.1) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2218\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2218\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> ) to the incident beam becomes completely plane-polarized, while light scattered at other intermediate angles is partially polarized.</p>\n<p>So, it is due to scattering of light by molecules in the atmosphere.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.44ex\" height=\"2.032ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -704 2846.6 898\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D444\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D444\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1068.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2346.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(C) Rainbow</b></p>\n<p>A rainbow is formed when sunlight enters a spherical raindrop suspended in the air. The process involves:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 1 Online Physics - Wave Optics Question 2 English Explanation\" src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1mpffsquo/8f2acd63-a92a-4da8-b039-ab18193e0369/5a25ef00-550c-11f1-8269-b952738c53c1/file-6y3zli1mpffsqup.webp?format=webp 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Refraction and Dispersion: Sunlight bends and splits into its constituent colors as it enters the drop due to different wavelengths having slightly different refractive indices.</p>\n<p>Reflection: The separated colors travel through the droplet and reflect off the back inside surface of the water drop.</p>\n<p>Refraction: The light exits the droplet, bending once more before reaching the observer's eye.</p>\n<p>So, during the process of rainbow formation the light undergoes dispersion and reflection.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.316ex\" height=\"1.595ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 2791.6 705\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-52\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1013.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2291.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><b>(D) Dark and bright fringes</b></p>\n<p>The regular alternation of dark and bright bands (fringes) is the pattern produced by the phenomena of wave interference and diffraction. When light encounters a small aperture, obstacle, or slit system, it bends around edges (diffraction) and superimposes, generating constructive (bright) and destructive (dark) regions.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the dark and bright fringes are formed by diffraction.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.11ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 2700.6 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D446\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D446\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(922.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2200.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.452ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"29.202ex\" height=\"2.149ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 12907.2 950\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(958.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2236.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"35\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-35\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2736.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(3181.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"51\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-51\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(4237,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(5514.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(6014.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(6459.4,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-52\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(7473.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(8751,0)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(9251,0)\"><use data-c=\"2C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2C\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(9695.7,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\"> </text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(600,0)\"><use data-c=\"53\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-53\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(11129.4,0)\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(12407.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>. \n<br><br>Thus, the correct option is (A).</p>"}},"timeAllotted":240000,"topic":"polarisation","yearKey":"jee-advanced-2026-paper-1-online","active":true}]}